[{:adr/repos "kotoba-lang/kotoba-native, kotoba-lang/kotoba-verifier, kotoba-lang/osaho, kotoba-lang/amu", :adr/related "kotoba-native ADR 0081, kotoba-native ADR 0002, kotoba-verifier ADR 0051, osaho ADR 0271, adr-2607198300-kotoba-execution-without-jvm-node-rust", :adr/status "accepted", :adr/id "adr-2609093000-a-command-needs-stdout-before-it-needs-a-repo", :adr/body "Owner instruction, 2026-09-09: build kotoba/amu native binaries that run as fast\nas Rust, one repo per command, in kotoba-lang. This ADR records what was\nMEASURED that day before any repo was created, because the request assumes a\nnative command binary is reachable today and three quarters of it is not.\n\n## What runs today (measured 2026-09-09, aarch64-macos, amu --jvm-free)\n\nReproduction, not values -- the values move:\n\n    A=orgs/kotoba-lang/amu/bin/amu\n    printf '(ns t (:export [main]))\\n(defn main [] :i64 ...)\\n' > /tmp/t.kotoba\n    $A compile /tmp/t.kotoba --jvm-free --target aarch64-macos --output /tmp/t.kexe\n    $A extract-native /tmp/t.kexe --symbol main --output /tmp/m.bin\n    cc -O2 -o /tmp/loader orgs/kotoba-lang/amu/tools/kexe_loader.c\n    /tmp/loader /tmp/m.bin <offset> <arity> aarch64 <allow-csv|->\n\n| face | state |\n|---|---|\n| .kotoba to machine code, executed | works |\n| fs/browse (wire 34), directory listing, executed | works |\n| fs/app-data (wire 35) read / write / ranged read | hosted by the loader |\n| env/read (wire 33) | hosted by the loader |\n| string-substring / string-byte-length / string-contains? | lower to native |\n| string-index-of | REFUSED, see below |\n| stdout, argv, exit status | wires 37, 38, 39 -- added 2026-09-10 |\n| standalone executable | scripts/package-command.cljs -- added 2026-09-10 |\n\nThe last two rows are what stands between this and a command. The loader is a\nHOST: it mmaps the code, answers capability calls, and prints the RETURN VALUE\nas an EDN map. There is no way for a guest to write a byte to stdout, read its\nown argv, or set an exit status, and `kotoba.native.macho` encodes a `.o`\nobject, not a linked executable. `capability-catalog.edn` lists 35 capabilities\nand none of them is stdout or argv -- so this is not a qualification gap in a\nkit, it is an absent capability.\n\n## The first blocker, and why it was the right one to take first\n\n`string-index-of` failed as `aggregate ABI rejected: call-abi-not-admitted`.\nWithout an offset a guest cannot cut a string at a separator, so it cannot\nsplit lines -- which removes grep, wc, cut, head, tail, sort and uniq at once,\nand is why `kbb.browse` could not compile for native even though the loader has\nhosted its provider all along.\n\nIt turned out to be one line of exposure, not a feature: `kotoba$string-find`,\nthe helper kotoba-native ADR 0002 already had, ALREADY answers the first\nmatching byte offset. `string-contains?` was folding that offset to 0/1 and\nthrowing it away.\n\nLanded as three changes, because there are three gates in front of it and ADR\n0002 measured that opening one alone moves nothing:\n\n  * kotoba-native `lower-index-of` + dispatch on both ISAs (its ADR 0081)\n  * kotoba-verifier `string-operations` gains `string-index-of 2` (its ADR 0051)\n  * osaho: the oracle itself was wrong (its ADR 0271)\n\n## The reference interpreter was the implementation that was wrong\n\nkotoba-native's string-search suite never writes an expected value down: each\nrow runs through `kotoba.kir/execute` twice, once as the operation and once as\nthe rewrite. Adding an astral row made that suite fail as `(not (= 7 4))` on\n`(string-index-of` G-clef + `ab, ab)`.\n\n`kotoba.kir.value/utf8-index-of!` walked the haystack one UTF-16 unit at a\ntime, charged a high surrogate 4 bytes, then stepped onto the LOW surrogate and\ncharged 3 more. Every astral code point before a match added 7. The JS emitter,\nthe new native lowering and CPython all answer 4.\n\nNothing had caught it because every multi-byte test row used 2- and 3-byte code\npoints, which are one UTF-16 unit each. The existing test even named the wrong\nanswer it was guarding against -- a UTF-16 answer of 6 -- and 7 is neither that\nnor the right one. It also reached artifacts, not just runtime: `lower` folds a\npure i64 entry through this evaluator, so a constant `string-index-of` over an\nastral literal was baked in wrong.\n\nThis is the workspace rule about oracles paying for itself. A suite that had\nwritten 4 down would have been GREEN and the reference would still be wrong.\n\n## Decisions\n\n1. Foundation before repos, each step proved by EXECUTING the artifact rather\n   than by building it. The remaining foundation is: argv, stdout/stderr and\n   exit status as capabilities with loader providers, then a linked executable\n   so `./ls` is a file rather than a blob plus a loader invocation.\n2. Naming is the ORIGIN plane. POSIX utilities are specified by IEEE Std\n   1003.1, so they take `org-ieee-<utility>`, following `org-ieee-tar`, which\n   states that pattern for exactly this reason. Non-POSIX entries in the\n   owner's list route to their own origin instead -- ssh is IETF and\n   `org-ietf-ssh` already exists.\n3. The command set is the 28 the owner listed. `tar` is already\n   `org-ieee-tar` (a format library, not yet a command surface); awk, sed,\n   curl and ssh are each a larger subject than the rest and are sequenced last.\n\n## Not done, and named so it is not mistaken for done\n\nNo command repo exists yet, and none can produce a runnable binary until argv,\nstdout and exit status exist. Do not scaffold 28 repos against a runtime that\ncannot print. The next step is the loader's provider table, not a repo.\n\n\n## Where this stands (2026-09-10)\n\nTwo rows in the table above read `do not exist` when this was written and\ndo not now. They were changed in place rather than left standing: a reader\nreaching that table needs the current position, and the history of why is\nin the prose around it.\n\n### The foundation decision 1 asked for is delivered\n\nThree capability wires, each with a loader provider, each carried through\nthe whole catalog chain -- kotoba-lang authority, kotoba-sema frontend map,\namu loader -- because a capability in the authority is invisible to the\ncompiler until the sema pin moves:\n\n    :io/write        37   bytes to fd 1, answered with the decimal count\n    :cli/args        38   empty request -> count, decimal -> that argument\n    :io/write-error  39   the same for fd 2\n\n:fs/app-data (35) also gained an EXISTS form, which is what lets a command\ntell `no such file` from `empty file` without trapping. Out of scope it\nanswers 0 rather than refusing, so probing cannot map the filesystem\noutside the grant.\n\nscripts/package-command.cljs turns an extracted blob into a standalone\nMach-O, so a command is a file rather than a loader invocation. Grant,\nfilesystem scope, browse scope, string arena, fuel and pairs are baked in\nat package time and are NOT movable by the caller.\n\n### The ceilings found by writing the commands\n\nEach measured rather than read off a document, and each shaped code:\n\n  * Five parameters. kotoba.compiler.frontend/max-parameters is 5, an ABI\n    arity limit rather than a language decision. head and tail pack an exit\n    status and a header flag into one word; wc computes its totals in a\n    SECOND walk, because three sums plus index, count and flag is seven.\n  * The wire carries UTF-8, not bytes. Every string result is validated as\n    canonical UTF-8, so cp cannot copy a file that is not -- it traps rather\n    than truncating. cp is otherwise a byte copier, so that is a real\n    restriction of domain and its README names it.\n  * No stdin, and no cwd. Every command refuses rather than pretending to\n    have read an empty stdin. pwd was deliberately NOT shipped: /bin/pwd -L\n    validates PWD against the real directory -- with PWD=/tmp it prints the\n    physical path anyway -- which needs getcwd. EXISTS cannot substitute,\n    since /tmp exists and is still the wrong answer. That is a :sys/cwd\n    wire, not a command.\n\n### Repos\n\nNINETEEN, each with a suite comparing stdout, stderr AND exit status\nagainst the system utility, and negative controls that fail for the reason\nthey name:\n\n    echo cat ls wc head tail find grep sort uniq cut cp sed\n    mkdir rm mv chmod du awk\n\nSix of those came after the first twelve because the LOADER grew, not\nbecause the language did. mkdir, rm, mv and chmod are the commands that\nchange a filesystem; du is the first consumer of a block count.\n\nWhat is still blocked, with the blocker named rather than the command\nlisted as \"todo\":\n\n  * pwd -- :sys/cwd (wire 40) is registered in the authority and in\n    kotoba-sema; the loader provider is written and held, see below\n  * chown -- privilege, not capability. A non-root process cannot give a\n    file away, so the command would be untestable rather than unbuilt\n  * ps, top, kill, xargs -- process spawn and signals\n  * df, free -- statfs and sysinfo\n  * curl, ssh -- network. :net/datagram is in the catalog with no native\n    provider; org-ietf-ssh exists on its own origin plane\n  * less -- interactive, needs a terminal\n  * tar -- org-ieee-tar is a format library with no command surface\n  * cd -- a shell builtin, not a binary at all\n  * ls -l -- needs nlink, uid, gid and mtime; STAT_SEP answers mode,\n    size, blocks and is-directory only\n\n### What the suites caught, which is the point of writing them\n\nSEVEN suites that could not have failed, in one day, each with a\ndifferent cause. This is the finding of the whole exercise:\n\n  1. a harness that dropped every operand past the second (grep)\n  2. one that dropped every operand past the first (sort)\n  3. zsh word splitting collapsing an argv into one string (echo)\n  4. a fixture name that was never a fixture, so both sides reported the\n     same missing file and agreed (uniq)\n  5. a rule that fitted the sibling but not the command -- chmod's MODE\n     mapped onto the fixture directory, so nothing succeeded\n  6. a FLAG mapped onto the fixture directory, so 72 flag cases were\n     green with no flag executing (sort)\n  7. a control whose patch never applied, reported as a clean run (du)\n\nEvery one was visible in the OUTPUT and not in the status: prefixes that\nnever appeared, lines from a file that was never read, a suite in which\nnothing succeeded. The status said green each time.\n\nTwo defences came out of it and both are now in use. A harness must pass\nflags and programs through VERBATIM and map only real operands to paths.\nAnd a control's patch must be verified to have landed before its result\nis believed -- org-ieee-awk's `verify-argv!` goes further and restates\nindependently what each argv must be, which catches the case where the\nconstruction rule itself is wrong.\n\nA CONTROL THAT PASSES IS A FINDING, NOT A RELIEF. It happened four times\nand each time the suite was weaker than it looked: sed's `g` scan (every\npattern was one byte, so advancing one byte and advancing past the match\nwere the same thing), grep's case fold (the fixture matched unfolded\nanyway), awk's separator scan (the miscounts cancelled), and awk's\noperand dropping (both sides got the same truncated vector, so they\nstill agreed -- agreement is not coverage).\n\nThe suites also caught defects in the commands they were added for:\ncut terminated an undelimited last line that /usr/bin/cut leaves\nunterminated, a single-operand bug only two operands made visible; uniq\nand cut and ls all keyed a flag off the argument COUNT, which stops\nbeing right the moment a second operand exists; and sort's `-r` exposed\nan append that lost a line consisting of one empty string, green for 25\ncases because ascending order never leaves that line alone.\n\n### Measurement corrected me twice\n\nNeither was caught by reasoning; both took a second measurement.\n\ncp does NOT give a new destination the source's mode. It gives\n`source & ~umask`. My original reading used 755 and 644, both\numask-invariant under 022, so it could not distinguish the two rules --\nand the difference matters: a straight copy of the source's mode hands\nout a world-writable file for a 666 or 777 source.\n\nawk's missing-file diagnostic is not one line but up to three, the\nmiddle carrying the PREVIOUS file's record count and absent entirely\nwhen nothing has been read yet. I had written \"one line\" into a brief\nas fact.\n\n### The loader identity is the sharpest edge in this work\n\nkototama-native hashes the reviewed loader source and refuses a\nmismatch, so changing kexe_loader.c without advancing kotoba-lang/\nartifact fails EVERY native execution -- 25 failures and 109 errors in\none suite, all one cause, reading as a tampered loader. The chain is\nartifact -> kototama-native -> amu, and it moved four times today.\n\nIts supervisor report compares key sets for EXACT equality, so the posix\nand windows loaders must agree on which keys a report has or one of them\nis rejected as malformed evidence. Adding `:string-pool` to one and not\nthe other took both windows jobs red while everything else was green.\n\nA docstring in that file broke on RAW QUOTES twice. The first reached\nmain and took four CI jobs red; the second was caught before landing by\na structural check that counts a `def`'s arguments -- reading the file\ndoes not discriminate, because the broken and fixed forms both parse as\nten forms.\n\nFour cases in one day where a test could not have failed: three harnesses\nthat dropped every operand past the first or second (grep, sort, and the\necho suite through zsh word splitting), and one naming a fixture that did\nnot exist, so both implementations agreed on the same missing-file error.\nEach was visible in the OUTPUT rather than the status -- prefixes that never\nappeared, lines from a file that was never read.\n\nThey also caught two real defects in the commands they were added for: cut\nterminated an undelimited last line that /usr/bin/cut leaves unterminated,\na single-operand bug only two operands made visible; and uniq keyed its -c\nflag off the argument COUNT, which stops being right the moment a second\noperand exists.\n\nThe loader identity is the other thing measured here. kototama-native\nhashes the reviewed loader source and refuses a mismatch, so changing\nkexe_loader.c without advancing kotoba-lang/artifact fails EVERY native\nexecution -- 25 failures and 109 errors in one suite, all one cause. Its\nsupervisor report compares key sets for exact equality, so the posix and\nwindows loaders must agree on which keys a report has or one of them is\nrejected as malformed evidence.", :adr/date "2026-09-09", :db/id -1, :adr/authority "owner instruction, 2026-09-09", :adr/title "A command needs stdout before it needs a repo, and the scan already knew the offset"}]
